<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
                       http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.20.xsd">

    <!--
      Repairs the data left behind by the bug fixed in EnvironmentServiceImpl.create():
      creating an environment did not create a FlagEnvironmentState for the flags the
      project already had, so every such (flag, environment) pair is missing its row.

      The consequence was silent: the SDK reads flags *through* this table, so those flags
      simply did not appear in GET /api/v1/sdk/flags for the new environment, and the admin
      API could not toggle them (updating a state requires the row to exist). The service
      fix only protects environments created from now on — without this backfill, every
      environment already created after its project had flags stays broken.

      Safe to re-run: the LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL only inserts pairs that have no row, and
      uq_flag_states_flag_env (migration 007) would reject a duplicate anyway.

      Archived flags are included deliberately, matching the service: they are still flags in
      the project, and skipping them would reopen the gap the moment one is unarchived.

      Defaults come from the table itself (007/008): enabled=false, rollout_percent=100,
      created_at/updated_at=now(). New rows start disabled — a repair must never turn a flag
      ON in an environment nobody has configured.
    -->
    <changeSet id="019-backfill-missing-flag-environment-states" author="dev">
        <sql>
            INSERT INTO flag_environment_states (feature_flag_id, environment_id)
            SELECT f.id, e.id
            FROM feature_flags f
            JOIN environments e ON e.project_id = f.project_id
            LEFT JOIN flag_environment_states s
                   ON s.feature_flag_id = f.id AND s.environment_id = e.id
            WHERE s.id IS NULL
        </sql>
        <!--
          Not reversible: the inserted rows are indistinguishable from legitimately created
          ones, so a rollback would have to guess which to delete. Deleting flag state is
          destructive and this migration only ever adds rows that should have existed all
          along, so an empty rollback is the safe contract.
        -->
        <rollback/>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
